\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\f0\fs36\lang9 Module 4: Essential Statistics & Mathematics for DataScience \par
 \par
\b0\fs28 Theoretical Assignments:\par
1. Explain Key Statistical Concepts\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj 2. Presentation: Importance of Probability in Machine Learning\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1  \b * Explain Key Statistical Concepts\par
1. \b0 Population and Sample\par
Population: The complete set of items or individuals under study.\par
Example: All students in a university.\par
\par
\b 2 \b0 .Sample: A subset of the population used for analysis.\par
Example: 100 students selected from the university\b .\par
\par
3. \b0 Variance and Standard Deviation\par
Variance: Measures how far data points are spread out from the mean.\par
Standard Deviation (SD): Square root of variance, gives spread in original units.\par
Higher SD = More spread in data.\par
\par
\b 4\b0 . Probability\par
Likelihood of occurrence of an event, ranges from 0 to 1.\par
Example: Tossing a fair coin:\par
P(Heads) = 0.5, P(Tails) = 0.5\par
\b 5. \b0 Correlation and Causation\par
Correlation: Statistical relationship between two variables (positive/negative).\par
Causation: One variable directly affects the other.\par
Important: Correlation does NOT imply causation.\par
\b 6\b0 . Hypothesis Testing\par
Method to test assumptions about a population.\par
Includes Null Hypothesis (H\f1\u8320?) and Alternative Hypothesis (H\u8321?).\par
Use p-value to decide:\par
If p < 0.05 \f2\u8594?\f1  \f0 Reject H\f1\u8320? (significant result)\par
If p > 0.05 \f2\u8594?\f1  \f0 Fail to reject H\f1\u8320?\par
\b 7\b0\f3 . \f1 Confidence\f3  \f1 Interval\par
Range\f3  \f1 of\f3  \f1 values\f3  \f1 within\f3  \f1 which\f3  \f1 the\f3  \f1 true\f3  \f1 population\f3  \f1 parameter\f3  \f1 is\f3  \f1 expected\f3  \f1 to\f3  \f1 fall\f3  \f1 with\f3  \f1 a\f3  \f1 certain\f3  \f1 confidence\f3  (\f1 commonly\f3  \f1 95%\f3 ).\f1\par
\par
\b 8\f3 . \b0\f1 Outliers\par
Data\f3  \f1 points\f3  \f1 significantly\f3  \f1 different\f3  \f1 from\f3  \f1 others\f3 , \f1 can\f3  \f1 affect\f3  \f1 results\f3 .\f1\par
Example\f3 : \f1 In\f3  [\f1 10\f3 , \f1 12\f3 , \f1 11\f3 , \f1 13\f3 , \f1 55\f3 ], \f1 55\f3  \f1 is\f3  \f1 an\f3  \f1 outlier\f3 .\f0\lang9\par
\b .* Presentation: Importance of Probability in Machine Learning\par

\pard\ri-1208\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0 Machine Learning (ML) involves making predictions and decisions based on data.Real-world data is uncertain and incomplete.Probability helps ML models handle uncertainty effectively.\par
\par
\b 2\b0 . Role of Probability in Machine Learning.Quantifies uncertainty,\f1  \f0 Helps make predictions with confidence scores\lang1033 ,\lang9 Forms the backbone of many algorithms,Enables handling of noisy or incomplete data\par
\par
\b 3. \b0 Algorithms Based on Probability,Naive Bayes Classifier: Uses Bayes\f1\rquote  \f0 Theorem to classify data.,Logistic Regression: Outputs probabilities for binary classification.,Hidden Markov Models (HMM): Used for sequential data like speech.Bayesian Networks: Represents probabilistic relationships among variables.\par
\par
\b 4. \b0 Example: Spam Email Detection\par
ML model calculates the probability that an email is spam based on words in the email.\par
If P(Spam | Features) > threshold \f2\u8594? Email marked as spam.\par
\par
\b 5\b0 . Probability Distributions in ML\par
Normal Distribution: Many natural processes follow this (e.g., heights, test scores).Bernoulli Distribution: For binary outcomes (yes/no, 0/1).Binomial Distribution: Count of successes in multiple trials.Poisson Distribution: Modeling rare events (e.g., number of website crashes per month).\par
\par
\b 6\b0 . Uncertainty Quantification\par
Probabilistic models not only predict but also estimate the certainty of predictions.\par
Example: Cat Image \u8594? 80% probability it's a cat, 20% it's a dog.\par
\par
\b 7. \b0 Evaluation with Probabilistic Metrics\par
ROC-AUC Curve: Assesses classifier performance.\par
Precision, Recall, F1-Score: Derived using probabilistic predictions.\par
Confidence intervals for model accuracy indicate reliability.\par
\par
