\b\fs36 7. A research study wants to analyze the weight distribution of a sample of\par
individuals to assess their health and body composition. Data: Let's consider the weights (in kilograms) of a sample of 100 individuals:\par
Weights:\par
55, 60, 62, 65, 68, 70, 72, 75, 78, 80, 82, 85, 88, 90, 92, 95, 100, 105, 110, 115, 120, 125, 130, 135, 140, 145, 150, 155, 160, 165, 170, 175, 180, 185, 190, 195, 200, 205, 210, 215, 220, 225, 230, 235, 240, 245, 250, 255, 260, 265, 270, 275, 280, 285, 290, 295, 300, 305, 310, 315, 320, 325, 330, 335, 340, 345, 350, 355, 360, 365, 370, 375, 380, 385, 390, 395, 400, 405, 410, 415, 420, 425, 430, 435, 440, 445, 450, 455, 460, 465, 470, 475, 480, 485, 490, 495, 500, 505, 510, 515\par
\par
\par
\fs32 a. Quartiles: Calculate the first quartile (Q1), median (Q2), and thirdquartile(Q3) of the weight distribution.\par
 \b0 a. Quartiles Calculation\par
First Quartile (Q1) \f1\emdash  25th Percentile:\par
Q1 = 170.0 kg\par
This means 25% of individuals weigh 170 kg or less.\par
\par
Second Quartile (Q2) \emdash  Median (50th Percentile):\par
Q2 = 285.0 kg\par
This means 50% of individuals weigh 285 kg or less, i.e., the median weight.\par
\par
Third Quartile (Q3) \emdash  75th Percentile:\par
Q3 = 400.0 kg\par
This means 75% of individuals weigh 400 kg or less.\f0\par
\b\par
 b. Percentiles: Calculate the 15th percentile, 50th percentile, and 85thpercentile of the weight distribution. \par
\b0 15th Percentile:\par
15th Percentile = 124.0 kg\par
This means 15% of individuals weigh 124 kg or less.\par
\par
50th Percentile (Median):\par
50th Percentile = 285.0 kg\par
As expected, same as the median.\par
\par
85th Percentile:\par
85th Percentile = 446.0 kg\par
This means 85% of individuals weigh 446 kg or less, or 15% weigh more than that.\par
\b\par
 c. Interpretation: Based on the quartiles and percentiles, what can be inferredabout the weight distribution of the individuals?\par
\fs36\par
\b0\fs24 The weight distribution ranges from 55 kg to 515 kg, with a fairly uniform spread.\par
The difference between Q1 (170 kg) and Q3 (400 kg) gives the Interquartile Range (IQR) of 230 kg, showing the middle 50% of individuals fall within this range.\par
The Median (285 kg) indicates the central tendency.\par
The 15th percentile (124 kg) and 85th percentile (446 kg) show that most individuals' weights fall between these values.\par
The data appears to be evenly distributed, with no strong skewness or clustering at one end.\par
